WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.529
The real life exploits of Leonidas of Sparta
and his 300 warriors at Thermoplyae have given

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:10.620
rise to the myth of the Spartan superhero
– the supremely disciplined man of few words

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:15.639
who had a body of steel, could endure any
hardship and would fight to his last breath.

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:20.500
Such men really did exist and chief among
them was Leonidas, the Spartan king who defied

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.270
the might of Persia, saving Greece from annihilation.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:30.810
In this week’s Biographics we discover the
real life Leonidas.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:46.690
Early Years
Leonidas was born around 540 BCE in the Greek

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:47.690
city of Sparta.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:52.699
At that time Greece was made up of hundreds
of city states, of which Athens and Sparta

00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:53.699
were the largest.

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:56.469
Leonidas’ father, Anaxandrides, was the
king of Sparta.

00:00:56.469 --> 00:01:01.899
For the Greeks, warfare was the supreme statement
of a citizen.

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:05.940
It is what made a man and gave him the right
to be a part of his city.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:10.840
Every Greek, in every city state was obliged
to military service from the age of twenty

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.300
until the age of forty-five.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:17.260
In order to prepare for that life, boys were
put into a military training camp, known as

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.590
the Agoge, from the age of seven.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:24.270
There was only exception to this compulsory
military training – the first-born son of

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.660
the king of each city state.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:30.260
His upbringing would be focused on grooming
him for the power that he would inherit on

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:31.460
his father’s death.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.340
Leonidas, however, was the third son of Anaxandrides.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:38.460
His older half-brother, Cleomenes I was first
in line to be the next king of Sparta.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.400
As a result, Leonidas spent his formative
years learning how to become a Spartan warrior.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:48.390
Between the age of seven and twelve, Leonidas
underwent a training regimen, along with a

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:53.340
group of other boys, under the supervision
of a warden who was called the paidonomos,

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.360
which literally translates to ‘herder of
boys.’

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.340
This was a highly respected Spartan, drawn
from the highest social class.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:06.020
His authority allowed him to punish any who
misbehaved, and to this end he was accompanied

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:10.969
by a whip wielding squad of youths who were
ready to eagerly mete out his punishments.

00:02:10.969 --> 00:02:16.489
As the system was designed to produce effective
warriors, great emphasis was placed on a rough

00:02:16.489 --> 00:02:17.840
and tumble lifestyle.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.340
The boys were divided into bands and they
chose as their leader the best fighter.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:26.230
Meanwhile the paidonomos would keep a close
eye on their antics, meting out punishment

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.349
to any who got out of line.

00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:31.170
Spartan boys in the Agoge were not permitted
to wear shoes.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:36.390
It was believed that leaping, jumping and
running were accomplished more swiftly barefoot.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.980
The boys were also allowed just one cloak
throughout the year.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:45.319
This was designed to force them to grow accustomed
to sweltering summers and freezing winters,

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:47.640
making do with what little covering they had.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:52.500
Food for the boys was strictly rationed, on
the belief that a lean, hungry man fights

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.390
far more effectively than a fat, satiated
one.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:59.690
Also, being able to go a long time without
food would make for a better prepared warrior.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:03.410
Theft was severely punished – unless it
involved the stealing of food.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:07.769
The logic behind this was that by learning
to pilfer food the boys would be practicing

00:03:07.769 --> 00:03:11.639
the skills of foraging that would be needed
when on military campaign.

00:03:11.639 --> 00:03:16.389
As well as their training to become warriors,
the boys were also taught to read and write.

00:03:16.389 --> 00:03:20.890
In fact, the Spartans gained a reputation
as men of learning, who loved music, poetry

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:21.890
and dance.

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.659
The age of twelve was a watershed year in
the life of a young Spartan.

00:03:25.659 --> 00:03:30.560
He was now placed in a band of about a dozen
of his peers, to live under the headship of

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.459
one drawn from his ranks, the eiren, or prefect.

00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:38.359
The one chosen was the smartest, the most
battle ready and cunning.

00:03:38.359 --> 00:03:42.640
Though there is no record that Leonidas was
put in this position, it is quite likely that

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:46.939
he, being the son of the king and given the
future battle prowess that he displayed, did

00:03:46.939 --> 00:03:50.170
indeed serve as an eiren during his teen years.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:53.980
The other boys had to give their absolute
obedience to the eiren.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:58.760
They were to act as his servants, collecting
firewood and fetching vegetables using their

00:03:58.760 --> 00:03:59.760
thieving skills.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.620
Also, at the age of twelve, Spartan boys were
taken under the wing of an erastes, or ‘admirer’,

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.489
to receive a form of mentoring.

00:04:06.489 --> 00:04:10.640
Whereas in other Grecian city states this
relationship was a sexual one, this was not

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.050
the case in Sparta.

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:16.669
Desire for the body rather than the soul of
a boy was considered to be the height of shame.

00:04:16.669 --> 00:04:21.739
At the age of eighteen, the Spartan youth
became a melleiren, a title which means ‘nearly

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:22.739
an eiren’.

00:04:22.739 --> 00:04:27.199
His military training was now stepped up as
he came close to entering military service.

00:04:27.199 --> 00:04:33.860
And so, by the age of twenty, Spartan society
had moulded Leonidas into an agile, battle-ready

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:38.669
military machine, full of self-confidence,
discipline and a killer instinct.

00:04:38.669 --> 00:04:41.780
He was ready to make his mark on the field
of battle.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.340
Leonidas had trained to be a hoplite warrior.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:49.580
Hoplites were experts in using the short iron
sword and round shield and spear.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:53.620
They fought in tight phalanx formation in
which they would approach the enemy with shield

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:59.340
overheads to form a tortoise-shell like defence
to the oncoming barrage of arrows.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:02.740
Just as Leonidas was about to enter military
service, news reached him that his father,

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:03.949
the King, had died.

00:05:03.949 --> 00:05:06.520
His brother, Cleomenes I now inherited the
throne.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:11.400
So, while one brother took on the mantle of
supreme rulership over the city-sate, the

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:16.690
other inherited the role that he had given
his entire life to – that of a Spartan warrior.

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:20.660
The Spartans army was divided into age groups,
spanning ten years.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:24.490
Within these divisions, the basic grouping
of men was into mess groups of about fifteen

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.490
men.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:28.259
It was with these men that Leonidas lived
during his twenties.

00:05:28.259 --> 00:05:32.440
It was also around this time that Leonidas
married the daughter of his half-brother Cleomones.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:36.870
She was named Gorgo and was by all accounts
a beautiful and politically astute woman.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:54.740
Leonidas’ older half-brother, Cleomones.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:59.850
Over the last three years, Grecian city states
had, one by one, succumbed to the invading

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:00.850
Persians.

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:05.139
The capitulation of these powers was a source
of outrage to the proud Spartan king and,

00:06:05.139 --> 00:06:11.000
in 491, he attempted to overthrow a neighbouring
king who was about to concede to the Persians.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.321
But when their king’s plans to oust a duly
appointed fellow king was revealed, the people

00:06:14.321 --> 00:06:18.389
of Sparta were not happy and Cleomones was
forced to flee the city.

00:06:18.389 --> 00:06:22.259
He then set about gathering a formidable army
in the surrounding territories.

00:06:22.259 --> 00:06:26.840
Wisely, the Spartans allowed him to return
but, believing that their king had gone insane,

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.560
had him thrown into chains.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:32.240
Apparently, this order was carried out on
the directions of Leonidas.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:36.130
Cleomones was placed under the guard of the
Helot slave class.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:40.110
The imprisoned king managed to persuade one
of his captors to lend him his dagger.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:44.979
With that he apparently committed an appalling
sort of hari-kari, slicing himself into pieces

00:06:44.979 --> 00:06:46.139
from his feet upward.

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:50.740
When Cleomones had taken the throne thirty
years previously, his half-brother Dorieus,

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:55.030
who was older than Leonidas, was incensed
that the throne had passed him by.

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:59.960
Finding it impossible to remain in Sparta,
he attempted to establish a colony in Africa.

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:03.660
When this failed, he went to Sicily where
he met an untimely death.

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:09.080
So, it was that, on the death of Cleomones
in 490, Leonidas was next in line for the

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.729
throne.

00:07:11.729 --> 00:07:21.840
The Persian Threat
Immediately upon ascending the throne, Leonidas

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:23.960
was thrown into the heat of conflict.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:29.180
For years the Persians had been threatening
Greece and now, the situation was at a critical

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:30.180
point.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:34.330
Swift action was needed – and Leonidas proved
to be the right man to deliver it.

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:38.050
The goal of the Persians was the absolute
subjection of Greece.

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:41.990
They already ruled over a number of Greek
cities on the coast of Asia.

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:46.409
The continued existence of other Greek city
states was a beacon of light for those already

00:07:46.409 --> 00:07:49.450
engulfed by the Persians, making them dream
of independence.

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:52.800
This was not acceptable to Persian king Xerxes.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.789
To crush all Grecian resistance, Xerxes has
gathered together a massive force, consisting

00:07:57.789 --> 00:08:03.820
of 1207 warships, and as many as 1, 700,000
warriors.

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:07.080
In stark contrast, the Greeks were badly disunited.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:11.699
Of more than one hundred city states, only
31 had agreed to stand and fight against the

00:08:11.699 --> 00:08:12.699
Persians.

00:08:12.699 --> 00:08:17.020
The kings of these 31 states met and swore
an oath to fight the Persians and to punish

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:19.440
any cities that went over to the Persian side.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.800
Still, with the key states of Thessaly and
Boiotia supporting Persia, there was no active

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:27.430
resistance to the invaders right down to the
city of Athens.

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:30.810
The Spartans had taken on the mantle of leadership
in the resistance.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:35.710
The city was recognised as being in a position
of moral, political and, above all, military

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:37.990
pre-eminence in all of Greece.

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:44.240
Under Leonidas, the Spartans were absolutely
determined to fight, to conquer or to die.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:49.079
The alliance with other resisting city-states
allowed Leonidas to put around 60,000 men

00:08:49.079 --> 00:08:50.079
into the field.

00:08:50.079 --> 00:08:54.950
King Xerxes of Persia believed that his overwhelming
superiority in numbers would guarantee the

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:55.950
victory.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:59.820
It was his strategy to keep his naval fleet
and his infantry closely connected.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:03.519
The infantry thus marched along the shore
and the Persians even dug a canal through

00:09:03.519 --> 00:09:07.440
Mount Athos to allow the ships to stay close
when the army got inland.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:11.920
By August 18th, 480, Xerxes had overrun Macedonia,
and Thessaly.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.670
His army had now arrived at the place called
Thermopylae, a narrow pass between Thessaly

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:17.720
and the ocean.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:21.899
Leonidas knew that he could never defeat the
entire Persian army.

00:09:21.899 --> 00:09:26.209
But he believed that if he could inflict a
devastating blow of such magnitude that it

00:09:26.209 --> 00:09:32.019
decimated a portion of that army, the psychological
damage would be such that the Persians would

00:09:32.019 --> 00:09:33.019
withdraw.

00:09:33.019 --> 00:09:36.720
But not everyone was convinced that this strategy
would work.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:42.070
Undermining Leonidas’ strategy was the oracle
of Apollo at Delphi, who was considered to

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:45.230
have a hotline to the Gods of Greece.

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:49.490
When the Spartans had approached the Oracle
asking whether they should resist the Persians

00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:54.140
the reply that came back was . . .
Either the king of Sparta must die or the

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:58.530
Spartans themselves must be conquered, for
nothing can stand before the might of the

00:09:58.530 --> 00:09:59.530
Persian king.

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:03.639
Each successive city state that approached
the Oracle was likewise given a premonition

00:10:03.639 --> 00:10:04.639
of doom.

00:10:04.639 --> 00:10:06.930
Still, Leonidas was not deterred.

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:11.779
He, and every Spartan warrior with him, would
give his last breath to keep the Persians

00:10:11.779 --> 00:10:13.320
out of their beloved city-state.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.250
Leonidas did want to meet the overwhelming
Persian force on the open field.

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:22.330
It was his intention to withdraw to the spot
where the Peloponnesian Peninsula joins the

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:24.149
land of Attica.

00:10:24.149 --> 00:10:27.500
This was a very narrow strip of land just
a mile and a half long.

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:31.920
Here he would set his men to building a defensive
wall behind which they would fight Xerxes’

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:32.920
army.

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:36.470
The problem with this plan was that it left
Athens completed without any protection.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:41.120
So, against his better judgement, Leonidas
agreed to take a small force up to Thermopylae

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:44.190
and to engage the Persians at the narrowest
point of the pass.

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:07.519
would be selected for the defence of Thermopylae
– and they would be led by he himself.

00:11:07.519 --> 00:11:11.910
Why were just 300 chosen and why these particular
300?

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:15.670
Firstly 300 was a manageable number for an
elite taskforce.

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:20.920
Secondly the figure 300 had strong symbolic
and practical overtones in Sparta, as it was

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:23.769
the fixed number of the regular royal bodyguard.

00:11:23.769 --> 00:11:27.519
The bodyguards were known as the hippeis meaning
cavalrymen, though in fact they served as

00:11:27.519 --> 00:11:32.300
infantrymen in the dead centre of the hoplite
phalanx, where the commanding king would be

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:33.300
stationed.

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:38.200
The three hundred hippeis were especially
selected in an intense completion from among

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:42.959
men in the ten youngest adult citizen year-classes,
aged between twenty and twenty-nine.

00:11:42.959 --> 00:11:49.010
Leonidas’ Thermopylae advance guard of 300,
however, was to be selected with one crucial

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:50.339
additional criterion.

00:11:50.339 --> 00:11:54.970
Besides being exceptionally brave, skilful
and patriotic, each of the chosen few must

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:56.670
also have a living son.

00:11:56.670 --> 00:12:01.089
This would ensure that a son would be in place
to carry on his father’s name.

00:12:01.089 --> 00:12:06.029
These heirs would then constitute an elite
within an elite, bursting with pride to emulate

00:12:06.029 --> 00:12:07.850
the feat of their late fathers.

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:12.450
In addition to the three hundred Spartans,
Leonidas took around 4,000 Peloponnesians

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:14.980
and around a thousand non-combatant Helots.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:19.500
It is clear that the Thermopylae 300 were
to be, in effect, a suicide squad.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:24.420
The pass at Thermopylae had a flat plain in
front of it, and that was where the Persians

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:25.420
were encamped.

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:29.200
The pass through the mountains to the sea
was only six feet wide at points.

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:33.399
At the point where the pass widened to fifty
feet, the Spartans set about repairing an

00:12:33.399 --> 00:12:36.990
existing wall and making it a much more defensible
location.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:41.990
After Xerxe’s vast Persian forces arrived
at the pass’ western end, a three of four

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:44.490
days’ delay occurred before he launched
his assault.

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:49.060
In this pre battle pause, it is reported that
Xerxes sent a message to Leonidas, demanding

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:51.420
that he hand over his arms.

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:55.660
The Spartan King replied in just two words
– Molon Labe!

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:58.220
“Come and get them yourself!”

00:12:58.220 --> 00:13:02.250
It was also during this lull before the storm
that Persian advance reconnaissance reported

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:06.470
back to Xerxes the strange behaviour they
saw among the Spartans.

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:11.410
Rather than being anxious of the coming attack,
the Greek warriors appeared supremely at ease.

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:15.350
They busied themselves with bathing, braiding
one another’s hair and dancing.

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:20.290
The Persians interpreted this as signs of
fear induced madness until a traitorous Greek

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:24.230
in their midst informed the king that the
Spartans were not mad.

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:29.590
What the Persian spies had seen were the actions
of proud heroes preparing themselves for their

00:13:29.590 --> 00:13:32.139
final life and death struggle.

00:13:32.139 --> 00:13:35.610
The person king first sent in the Medes, a
force of about two thousand.

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:38.300
But these forces proved no match for the Spartans.

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:41.850
They wore neither helmets nor greaves and
their shields were made of wickerwork.

00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:46.100
In the confined spaces of the pass, they were
unable to take advantage of their superiority

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:47.100
in numbers.

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:51.260
When the Medes unleashed a hail of arrows
on the defenders, in unison the Spartans lifted

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:53.961
their shields overhead to form a defensive
roof over their heads.

00:13:53.961 --> 00:13:57.480
The Medes and the ranks of troops that followed
them did the best they possibly could under

00:13:57.480 --> 00:13:59.120
the judgemental eye of their king.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:02.410
But it was not enough, and they suffered heavy
losses.

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:06.470
In fact, losses were so great that, on three
occasions, Xerxes is said to have leapt up

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:11.480
in horror from his specially constructed throne,
appalled at the carnage and slaughter of some

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:12.560
of his best men.

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:16.579
From behind the protection of the refurbished
Phaeacian wall Leonidas’ men resisted by

00:14:16.579 --> 00:14:17.630
fighting in relays.

00:14:17.630 --> 00:14:22.630
This clever tactic maximized the efficient
output of their limited resources and energies.

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:27.339
Leonidas also managed to pull off a series
of feigned retreats followed by a sudden about

00:14:27.339 --> 00:14:32.130
turn, then a murderous onslaught on their
overconfident and distorted pursuers.

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:37.079
Eventually, towards the end of daylight, Xerxes
felt that he had no option but to send in

00:14:37.079 --> 00:14:42.290
his elite royal bodyguard, the ten thousand
Immortals, under the command of Hydarnes.

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:47.790
But, once again the attack was to no avail
– and it came at the cost of serious casualties.

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:52.440
The gore and bloodshed that engorged the narrow
mountain pass was overwhelming.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:57.920
The piles of quickly rotting corpses mounted,
the flies swarmed and the stench was palpable.

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:02.509
The battle raged into a second day, and still
Xerxes was unable to make a breakthrough.

00:15:02.509 --> 00:15:07.470
By now some 20,000 Persians had fallen to
the Greek resistance and the demoralizing

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:11.949
effect on the remains of the Persian army
was telling – why could they not turn back

00:15:11.949 --> 00:15:15.430
these few rebels?

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:25.740
Betrayal and Death
With his frustration at boiling point Xerxes

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:31.209
finally received his break, not through his
military superiority but by means of a traitor.

00:15:31.209 --> 00:15:35.319
Xerxes was approached by a Greek turn-coat
by the name of Ephialtes from the city of

00:15:35.319 --> 00:15:38.370
Malis who was very familiar with the paths
crossing the mountain range.

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:42.690
He told the king of another path through the
mountain that would allow the Persians to

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:45.180
bypass the Greek defensive position.

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:49.850
Ephialtes offered to escort as many as 10,000
Persian troops through that path by night

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:52.080
and turn the flank of the Spartans.

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:55.829
The Spartans knew of the existence of this
alternative route, but they didn’t know

00:15:55.829 --> 00:15:57.279
that Xerxes knew about it.

00:15:57.279 --> 00:16:01.610
Xerxes gave this special mission to members
of the royal guard of Immortals, and led by

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:05.949
Ephialtes, they set out in silence at nightfall,
aided by a full moon.

00:16:05.949 --> 00:16:10.949
After a heroic climb through the Anopaea mountain
range, up to 1,000 metres, they easily brushed

00:16:10.949 --> 00:16:15.540
off and bypassed the thousand Phaeacian guards
posted by Leonidas.

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:19.120
These troops were ill-prepared and were taken
completely by surprise.

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:24.740
Leonidas has long been blamed for not posting
a stronger reinforcement of the Anopea path.

00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:28.250
By the morning of day 3 of the conflict, the
Persians had the Greeks outflanked.

00:16:28.250 --> 00:16:32.319
Leonidas was now in a pincer grip, caught
from the rear as well as the front.

00:16:32.319 --> 00:16:36.709
It is at this point that Leonidas, having
recognized the hopelessness of the situation,

00:16:36.709 --> 00:16:40.790
gathered his forces together and ordered all
of the troops, except the Spartans, to make

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:41.790
their escape.

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:43.889
Their cities would need them to fight another
day.

00:16:43.889 --> 00:16:47.840
But the Spartans were to make their last stand
there at Thermopylae.

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:51.769
All melted away, except for a group of about
four hundred from Thesbes.

00:16:51.769 --> 00:16:56.290
When asked by the king why they, too, did
not go, they replied that they have stayed

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:58.029
because the Spartans have stayed.

00:16:58.029 --> 00:16:59.269
They would die with them.

00:16:59.269 --> 00:17:02.269
The Greeks made their last stand mostly outside
the Middle Gate wall, enabling them to close

00:17:02.269 --> 00:17:03.269
directly with the oncoming enemy.

00:17:03.269 --> 00:17:05.750
Leonidas showed himself a true Spartan by
the words with which allegedly ordered his

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:07.250
men to take their early morning meal before
the final encounter . . .

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:09.760
This evening, we shall dine in Hades.

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:12.540
The Spartans sold their souls at a heavy cost.

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:17.170
The Persian losses at the beginning of Day
3 were reportedly heavier even than those

00:17:17.170 --> 00:17:19.289
sustained on the previous two days.

00:17:19.289 --> 00:17:23.760
The Greeks, depleted though their numbers
were, repeatedly drove the Persians back.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:28.809
Then, inevitably, the 10, 000 of the Persian
Royal Guard of Immortals emerged from the

00:17:28.809 --> 00:17:29.809
rear.

00:17:29.809 --> 00:17:31.980
Leonidas fought and died like a man possessed.

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:36.310
When his word shattered after one too many
death blows, he used his hands and mouth to

00:17:36.310 --> 00:17:38.380
inflict injury upon the enemy.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:43.480
The exact manner of his death is unrecorded,
but we do know that, as soon as he fell, the

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:46.559
Persians took hold of the body and tried to
drag it away.

00:17:46.559 --> 00:17:50.580
Seeing this, the Spartans leapt on their king
and attempted to pull him back.

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:54.640
Four times the body of Leonidas was dragged
away before the remnant of the Spartan defenders

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:58.470
got full possession of it and formed a defensive
circle around it.

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:03.490
In this position, with their spears and lances
broken, their bodies battered and bloodied,

00:18:03.490 --> 00:18:07.690
and their dead king in their midst, the Spartans
resorted to throwing rocks at the surrounding

00:18:07.690 --> 00:18:09.000
force of Persians.

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:14.100
Finally, a massive hail of arrows was launched
and the last Spartans were cut down.

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:19.500
Perhaps the greatest testament to the effectiveness
of the heroic defence which Leonidas had affected

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:23.230
against the Persians was the treatment of
his corpse by Xerxes.

00:18:23.230 --> 00:18:27.160
The Spartan king was decapitated and his body
was crucified.

00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:30.640
Xerxes then went about trying to hide the
true number of casualties that his men had

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:31.640
suffered.

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:34.909
But the great defence by the Spartans could
not be hidden from history.

00:18:34.909 --> 00:18:38.860
The deaths of Leonidas and his fellow Spartans
greatly lifted Greek morale.

00:18:38.860 --> 00:18:42.990
And the vital period that the Spartans had
held up the Persian advance gave time for

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:47.530
the Athenian Greek fleet to wreak havoc on
the Persian navy and eventually led to the

00:18:47.530 --> 00:18:49.159
defeat of the Persian threat.

00:18:49.159 --> 00:19:07.440
So, by giving his life so dearly at Thermopylae,
Leonidas became the saviour 

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:26.899
of his people.

